  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the kitchen of Restaurant Victor, Chef Barinov discovers a promotional flyer announcing that hotel guests can summon the head chef directly to their rooms. Listen to his clash with manager Mikhail Dzhekovich.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "lev",
      "t": "Я пытался его предупредить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошёл вон.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это что за ерунда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "В нашем отеле действует новая услуга.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Это услуга для наших гостей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Вы сможете посмотреть на работу шефа.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Это не прерывает ваш отдых.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Я принимаю это после завтрака.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "А сейчас извини.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты введи новую услугу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Управляющий идёт прямо в номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не мешай нашему отдыху.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lev",
      "t": "Я пытался его предупредить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I tried to warn him.' Notice the reflexive suffix on the verb meaning 'tried', turning the action back on the subject. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошёл вон.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get out.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'went', while 'over there' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это что за ерунда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What kind of nonsense is this?' The grammar packs 'this' and 'nonsense' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "В нашем отеле действует новая услуга.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A new service operates in our hotel.' Notice how Russian expresses 'our' and 'service' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Это услуга для наших гостей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is a service for our guests.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'this' before landing on 'guests'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Вы сможете посмотреть на работу шефа.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will be able to watch the chef's work.' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'proper name' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Это не прерывает ваш отдых.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This does not interrupt your rest.' Mikhail chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'rest' to keep a polite distance.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Я принимаю это после завтрака.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I accept that after breakfast.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'breakfast' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "А сейчас извини.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And right now excuse me.' The meaning hinges on 'and' working together with 'sorry' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты введи новую услугу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You should introduce a new service.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'you' and finishing on 'service'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Управляющий идёт прямо в номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The hotel manager goes straight to the room.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'hotel manager' and finishing on 'number'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не мешай нашему отдыху.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not disturb our rest.' The grammar packs 'not' and 'to the rest' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
